{
  "dom_id": "step-e3842",
  "name": "Example: Finding the Correct Quadrant for Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation",
  "problem": "The angle $\\theta =  \\dfrac {8\\pi}3$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\dfrac {8\\pi}3$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0, 2\\pi).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the angle $\\dfrac {2\\pi}3$ is coterminal with $\\dfrac {8\\pi}3.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14223930/assets/2360ed202deeca403c4631c319b69df30a9f0268b7ef270a8e436103c0ac3edb.png)\n\nWe see that $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}$ also lies in this quadrant.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
